<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gundobad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 516</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gundobad was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> from about 473 to 516, after serving as a Roman general and briefly as kingmaker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he raised Glycerius to the throne in 473. In his kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley he issued the law code known as the Lex Burgundionum for his Burgundian subjects and a separate Roman code for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-romans">Gallo-Romans</a>. He remained an Arian but tolerated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gundobad.jpg" alt="Gundobad" width="320" height="595" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Codex · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gondebaud_par_Roger_Ferrier_Place_du_bourg_de_Four.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gundobad, born about 452, was the son of the Burgundian king Gundioc and the nephew of Ricimer, who had been the power behind the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. When Ricimer died on 18 August 472, Gundobad took the title of patrician. According to John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, he had earlier executed the deposed emperor Anthemius on his uncle's orders. He raised the comes domesticorum Glycerius to the western throne, then left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> after his father's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The early decades of the reign are poorly known; the main source, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, wrote almost a century later. He says Gundobad rid himself of his brothers, killing Gundomar and then Chilperic, whose wife he had drowned and whose daughters he exiled. One of them, Clotilde, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, though Danuta Shanzer and Ian Wood read a letter of Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> as showing that she was offered as an act of diplomacy.</p><p>The earliest firm date is 490, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> invaded north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> fought over Pavia, devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and carried off captives. Bishop Epiphanius of Pavia ransomed more than six thousand of them. Gundobad's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godegisel">Godegisel</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> against him, and Gundobad was defeated and pursued to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, where he bought peace with a yearly tribute; Marius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventicum">Avenches</a> dates this to 500.</p><p>Gundobad later broke the promise, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godegisel">Godegisel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> and, according to Gregory, killed him in 501 in an Arian church. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> died in 511 the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> favoured Gundobad and gave him the title magister militum. He died in 516 and was succeeded by his son Sigismund.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Lex Burgundionum, which combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> with Germanic custom, is attributed to Gundobad, though doubts surround its date. L. R. deSalis proposed a text without a date, which would better suit Sigismund's reign, and Katherine Fisher Drew holds that a core of the code came from Gundobad or his chancellery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, a Nicene bishop, answered an Arian's religious questions in several letters, showing considerable tolerance; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> later thought Gundobad had secretly become a Nicene Christian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gundobad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Glycerius, proclaimed emperor of the West in March 473 by the magister militum <b>Gundobad</b> and not recognised by the Eastern emperor Leo I, was forced to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-24">24 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by the Burgundian general <b>Gundobad</b>, the court official Glycerius was proclaimed emperor at Ravenna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-3">3 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Gundobad</b> issued a law code for his Burgundian subjects and another for Romans, and Burgundians and Gallo-Romans shared land.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-burgundians">Kingdom of the Burgundians</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=506" aria-label="Gaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gundobad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5068 799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5068 799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=506">What was here in 506 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
